    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. The 3 existing tests pass, and I ran it against a real stdio `MCPServer` through `opsbot.connection.open_session`. I couldn't delete my two test scripts, though \u2014 you'll need to do that yourself (below).\n\n```python\ndef _text_of(result: CallToolResult) -> str:\n    return \"\\n\".join(block.text for block in result.content if isinstance(block, TextContent))\n\n\nasync def run_tool(session: ClientSession, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any]) -> Any:\n    result = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\n    if result.is_error:\n        raise ToolCallFailed(name, _text_of(result))\n    # Structured content may be any JSON value (e.g. 0, [], \"\"), so test for None, not falsiness.\n    if result.structured_content is not None:\n        return result.structured_content\n    return _text_of(result)\n```\n\n**How it works with mcp 2.2.0:**\n- The result fields are `is_error`, `structured_content` and `content`, imported from `mcp.types`.\n- Structured output can be any JSON value, so the check is `is not None`. That way a structured `0` or `[]` is still returned as a value.\n- Blocks that aren't text (images, resources and so on) are skipped when joining the text.\n- No new dependencies.\n\n**What the run against a real server showed:**\n- A tool that raised on the server gave `ToolCallFailed(\"boom\", \"Error executing tool boom\")`. That message is exactly the text the server sent back; the server hides the original exception message.\n- A tool returning `-> str` came back as `{'result': 'hi'}`. MCPServer wraps plain return types as structured output, and as you specified, that structured value is returned as-is. If you'd rather get the plain text for tools like these, that needs a change to the rule.\n- A tool returning a bare `-> dict` has no output schema, so it has no structured output and came back as its JSON text.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my attempt to remove the test scripts was blocked by permissions. Please delete `_scratch_server.py` and `_scra"
